# >>> atlas-managed — `atlas workflow sync` owns this block; do not edit inside >>>
# Normalise to LF so generated/hashed files (e.g. topology/family.md, the CLI's byte-pins)
# match on every platform — atlas's `--check` freshness gates depend on it. This is the
# error-floor rule `atlas process check` requires of every member's .gitattributes.
* text=auto eol=lf
# PowerShell stays CRLF for native Windows execution.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
# <<< atlas-managed <<<

# Source stays LF in every checkout, so gofmt -l, cargo fmt, diffs and the conformance
# harness's byte comparisons mean the same thing on Windows and Linux.
* text=auto eol=lf
*.go text eol=lf
*.rs text eol=lf
*.ts text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
*.txt text eol=lf
go.mod text eol=lf
go.sum text eol=lf
Cargo.lock text eol=lf

# The corpus the four implementations are compared against byte for byte. A CRLF here would
# make one implementation disagree with the others on a checkout, which is exactly the
# failure this repository exists to detect, and it would be an artefact, not a real one.
vectors/** text eol=lf

# .mjs is left to detection on purpose: tools/conformance/run.mjs contains a literal NUL (a
# key separator), so git detects it as binary and stores it byte for byte. Forcing *.mjs to
# text would rewrite that file's line endings on the next commit.

*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
